an4.35:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest at the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
an4.35:1.2 | S: Then Vassakāra the brahmin, a chief minister of Magadha, went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Vassakāra the brahman, the chief minister of Magadha, went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.35:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
an4.35:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, when someone has four qualities we describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: “Master Gotama, we declare a person endowed with four qualities to be one of great discernment, a great man.
an4.35:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.35:2.3 | S: They are very learned in diverse fields of learning. | T: “There is the case where he is learned.
an4.35:2.4 | S: They understand the meaning of diverse statements, saying: ‘This is what that statement means; that is what this statement means.’ | T: “Whatever he hears, he immediately understands the meaning of the statement, ‘This is the meaning of that statement. This is the meaning of that statement.’
an4.35:2.5 | S: They are mindful, able to remember and recollect what was said and done long ago. | T: “He is mindful, able to remember & recollect what was done & said a long time ago.
an4.35:2.6 | S: They are deft and tireless in household duties, understanding how to go about things in order to complete and organize the work. | T: “He is adept in the affairs of the household life, vigorous, endowed with enough quick-wittedness in their techniques to organize them and get them done.
an4.35:2.7 | S: When someone possesses these four qualities we describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: “Master Gotama, we declare a person endowed with these four qualities to be one of great discernment, a great man.
an4.35:2.8 | S: If the worthy Gotama agrees with me, please say so. | T: If you think that I am worthy of agreement, Master Gotama, may you agree with me.
an4.35:2.9 | S: If he disagrees, please say so.” | T: If you think I am worthy of criticism, criticize me.”
an4.35:3.1 | S: “Brahmin, I neither agree nor disagree with you, | T: “I neither agree with you, brahman, nor do I criticize you.
an4.35:3.2 | S: but when someone has four qualities I describe him as a great man with great wisdom. | T: I declare a person endowed with four qualities to be one of great discernment, a great man.
an4.35:3.3 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.35:3.4 | S: It’s when someone practices for the welfare and happiness of the people. | T: “There is the case, brahman, where he practices for the welfare & happiness of many people and has established many people in the noble method, i.e., the rightness of what is admirable, the rightness of what is skillful.
an4.35:3.5 | S: They’ve established many people in the noble system, that is, the principles of goodness and skillfulness. | T: (none)
an4.35:3.6 | S: They think what they want to think, and don’t think what they don’t want to think. | T: “He thinks any thought he wants to think, and doesn’t think any thought he doesn’t want to think.
an4.35:3.7 | S: They consider what they want to consider, and don’t consider what they don’t want to consider. | T: He wills any resolve he wants to will, and doesn’t will any resolve he doesn’t want to will.
an4.35:3.8 | S: Thus they have achieved mental mastery of the paths of thought. | T: He has attained mastery of the mind with regard to the pathways of thought.
an4.35:3.9 | S: They get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when they want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: “He attains—whenever he wants, without strain, without difficulty—the four jhānas that are heightened mental states, pleasant abidings in the here & now.
an4.35:3.10 | S: They realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: “With the ending of effluents—he remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized them for himself right in the here & now.
an4.35:3.11 | S: Brahmin, I neither agree nor disagree with you, | T: “I neither agree with you, brahman, nor do I criticize you.
an4.35:3.12 | S: but when someone possesses these four qualities I describe him as a great man with great wisdom.” | T: I declare a person endowed with these four qualities to be one of great discernment, a great man.”
an4.35:4.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Gotama, it’s amazing! | T: “It’s amazing, Master Gotama, and astounding,
an4.35:4.2 | S: How well said this was by the worthy Gotama! | T: how well that has been said by Master Gotama.
an4.35:4.3 | S: And we will remember the worthy Gotama as someone who possesses these four qualities. | T: And I hold Master Gotama to be endowed with these same four qualities.
an4.35:4.4 | S: For the worthy Gotama practices for the welfare and happiness of the people … | T: “Master Gotama practices for the welfare & happiness of many people
an4.35:4.5 | P: bahu te janatā ariye ñāye patiṭṭhāpitā, yadidaṁ kalyāṇadhammatā kusaladhammatā. | T: and has established many people in the noble method, i.e., the rightness of what is admirable, the rightness of what is skillful.
an4.35:4.6 | P: Bhavañhi gotamo yaṁ vitakkaṁ ākaṅkhati vitakketuṁ taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakketi, yaṁ vitakkaṁ nākaṅkhati vitakketuṁ na taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakketi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ ākaṅkhati saṅkappetuṁ taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappeti, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ nākaṅkhati saṅkappetuṁ na taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappeti. | T: “Master Gotama thinks whatever thought he wants to think, and doesn’t think any thought he doesn’t want to think. He wills any resolve he wants to will, and doesn’t will any resolve he doesn’t want to will.
an4.35:4.7 | S: The worthy Gotama has achieved mental mastery of the paths of thought. | T: He has attained mastery of the mind with regard to the pathways of thought.
an4.35:4.8 | S: The worthy Gotama gets the four absorptions … when he wants, without trouble or difficulty. | T: “Master Gotama attains—whenever he wants, without strain, without difficulty—the four jhānas that are heightened mental states, pleasant abidings in the here & now.
an4.35:4.9 | S: The worthy Gotama has realized the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. He lives having realized it with his own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.” | T: “Master Gotama, with the ending of effluents remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized them for himself right in the here & now.”
an4.35:5.1 | S: “Your words are clearly invasive and intrusive, brahmin. | T: “Well, brahman, you are certainly being rude & presumptuous in making this statement,
an4.35:5.2 | S: Nevertheless, I will answer you. | T: but still I will respond to you.
an4.35:5.3 | S: For I do practice for the welfare and happiness of the people … | T: “Yes, brahman, I practice for the welfare & happiness of many people
an4.35:5.4 | P: bahu me janatā ariye ñāye patiṭṭhāpitā, yadidaṁ kalyāṇadhammatā kusaladhammatā. | T: and have established many people in the noble method, i.e., the rightness of what is admirable, the rightness of what is skillful.
an4.35:5.5 | P: Ahañhi, brāhmaṇa, yaṁ vitakkaṁ ākaṅkhāmi vitakketuṁ taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakkemi, yaṁ vitakkaṁ nākaṅkhāmi vitakketuṁ na taṁ vitakkaṁ vitakkemi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ ākaṅkhāmi saṅkappetuṁ taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappemi, yaṁ saṅkappaṁ nākaṅkhāmi saṅkappetuṁ na taṁ saṅkappaṁ saṅkappemi. | T: “And, yes, I think whatever thought I want to think, and don’t think any thought I don’t want to think. I will any resolve I want to will, and don’t will any resolve I don’t want to will.
