an7.53:3.9 | S: Then that will also be your offering to me as your guest.” | T: That will also be my guest gift.”
an7.53:4.1 | S: And when the night had passed the lay woman Nanda’s Mother had delicious fresh and cooked foods prepared in her own home. | T: So Nandamātar, as the night was ending, prepared exquisite staple & non-staple foods in her home.
an7.53:4.2 | S: Then the Saṅgha of mendicants headed by Sāriputta and Moggallāna arrived at Veḷukaṇṭa. | T: Then the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna arrived at Veḷukaṇṭaka before breakfast.
an7.53:4.3 | S: Then Nanda’s Mother addressed a man, | T: Nandamātar addressed a man,
an7.53:4.4 | S: “Please, my man, go to the monastery and announce the time to the Saṅgha, saying: | T: “Come, my good man. Having gone to the monastery, announce the time (of the meal) to the Saṅgha of monks, (saying,)
an7.53:4.5 | S: ‘Sirs, it’s time. The meal is ready in the home of the lady Nanda’s Mother.’” | T: ‘It’s time, venerable sirs. The meal is ready at Lady Nandamātar’s home.’”
an7.53:4.6 | S: “Yes, ma’am,” that man replied, and he did as she said. | T: Responding, “As you say, lady,” to Nandamātar, the man went to the monastery and announced the time to the Saṅgha of monks,
an7.53:4.7 | P: “kālo, bhante, ayyāya nandamātuyā nivesane niṭṭhitaṁ bhattan”ti. | T: “It’s time, venerable sirs. The meal is ready at Lady Nandamātar’s home.”
an7.53:4.8 | S: And then the Saṅgha of mendicants headed by Sāriputta and Moggallāna robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, went to the home of Nanda’s Mother, where they sat on the seats spread out. | T: Then early in the morning, having adjusted their under robes and carrying their robes & bowls, the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna went to Nandamātar’s home. On arrival, they sat down on seats laid out.
an7.53:4.9 | S: Then Nanda’s Mother served and satisfied them with her own hands with delicious fresh and cooked foods. | T: Nandamātar, with her own hands, served & satisfied the Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna with exquisite staple & non-staple food.
an7.53:5.1 | S: When Sāriputta had eaten and washed his hand and bowl, Nanda’s Mother sat down to one side. | T: Then, when Ven. Sāriputta had finished his meal and had rinsed his bowl & hands, Nandamātar, taking a low seat, sat to one side.
an7.53:5.2 | S: Sāriputta said to her, | T: As she was sitting there, Ven. Sāriputta said to her,
an7.53:5.3 | S: “Nanda’s Mother, who told you that the Saṅgha of mendicants was about to arrive?” | T: “Nandamātar, who informed you of the arrival of the Saṅgha of monks?”
an7.53:6.1 | S: “Sir, last night I rose at the crack of dawn and recited the verses of ‘The Way to the Far Shore’, and then I fell silent. | T: “Just now, venerable sir, after having risen as night approached dawn and having chanted the Pārāyana, I fell silent.
an7.53:6.2 | S: Then the great king Vessavaṇa, knowing I had finished, applauded me, | T: Then the great king Vessavaṇṇa, knowing that my chant was ended, applauded me:
an7.53:6.3 | S: ‘Good, sister! Good, sister!’ | T: ‘Excellent, sister! Excellent!’
an7.53:6.4 | S: I asked: ‘But who might you be, dearest?’ | T: “‘But who’s there, O One of the Auspicious Face?’
an7.53:6.5 | S: ‘Sister, I am your brother Vessavaṇa, the great king.’ | T: “‘I’m your brother, sister: the great king Vessavaṇṇa.’
an7.53:6.6 | S: ‘Good, dearest! Then may my recital of the teaching be my offering to you as my guest.’ | T: “‘Excellent, O One of the Auspicious Face! Excellent! Then let this Dhamma sequence that I’ve chanted be your guest gift.’
an7.53:6.7 | S: ‘Good, sister! And let this also be your offering to me as your guest. | T: “‘Excellent, sister! And let this also be my guest gift.
an7.53:6.8 | S: Tomorrow, the mendicant Saṅgha headed by Sāriputta and Moggallāna will arrive at Veḷukaṇṭa before breakfast. When you’ve served the Saṅgha, please dedicate the religious donation to me. | T: Tomorrow a Saṅgha of monks headed by Sāriputta & Moggallāna will be coming to Veḷukaṇṭaka before breakfast. Having served them, dedicate the offering to me.
an7.53:6.9 | S: Then that will also be your offering to me as your guest.’ | T: That will also be my guest gift.’
an7.53:6.10 | S: And so, sir, may the merit and the flourishing of merit of this gift be for the happiness of the great king Vessavaṇa.” | T: So in other words, may the merit of this gift be for the happiness of the great king Vessavaṇṇa.”
an7.53:7.1 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding,
an7.53:7.2 | S: that you converse face to face with a mighty and illustrious godling like the great king Vessavaṇa.” | T: that you would converse face to face with the great king Vessavaṇṇa, the young deva of such great power & such great might!”
an7.53:8.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:8.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:8.3 | S: I had an only son named Nanda who I loved dearly. | T: I had an only son: Nanda, dear & appealing.
an7.53:8.4 | S: The rulers forcibly abducted him on some pretext and had him executed. | T: The rulers seized & abducted him on some pretext and had him executed.
an7.53:8.5 | S: But I can’t recall getting upset when my boy was under arrest or being arrested, imprisoned or being put in prison, killed or being killed.” | T: But when the boy had been arrested or was being arrested, when he had been imprisoned or was being imprisoned, when he had been killed or was being killed, I don’t recall any alteration of my mind.”
an7.53:8.6 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding,
an7.53:8.7 | S: that you purify even the arising of a thought.” | T: that you can purify even as little as the arising of a thought.”
an7.53:9.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:9.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:9.3 | S: When my husband passed away he was reborn in one of the realms of spirits. | T: When my husband died, he reappeared in the yakkha realm.
an7.53:9.4 | S: He revealed to me his previous incarnation. | T: He appeared to me in his earlier bodily form,
an7.53:9.5 | S: But I can’t recall getting upset on that account.” | T: but I don’t recall, from that cause, any alteration of my mind.”
an7.53:9.6 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding,
an7.53:9.7 | S: that you purify even the arising of a thought.” | T: that you can purify even as little as the arising of a thought.”
an7.53:10.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:10.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:10.3 | S: Ever since we were both young, and I was given in marriage to my husband, I can’t recall betraying him even in thought, still less in deed.” | T: I was given in marriage to my young husband when I was a young girl, but I don’t recall ever transgressing against him in mind, much less in body.”
an7.53:10.4 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding,
an7.53:10.5 | S: that you purify even the arising of a thought.” | T: that you can purify even as little as the arising of a thought.”
an7.53:11.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:11.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:11.3 | S: Ever since I declared myself a lay follower, I can’t recall deliberately breaking any precept.” | T: Since declaring myself a lay follower, I don’t recall ever deliberately overstepping any training rule.”
an7.53:11.4 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing!” | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding!”
an7.53:12.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:12.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:12.3 | S: Whenever I want, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, I enter and remain in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: To whatever extent I want, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, I enter & remain in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an7.53:12.4 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, I enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, I enter & remain in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
an7.53:12.5 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, I enter and remain in the third absorption, where I meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading of rapture, I remain equanimous, mindful, & alert, and sense pleasure with the body. I enter & remain in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, one has a pleasant abiding.’
an7.53:12.6 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain, and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, I enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness.” | T: With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of joy & distress—I enter & remain in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.”
an7.53:12.7 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing!” | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding!”
an7.53:13.1 | S: “Sir, this is not my only incredible and amazing quality; | T: “I don’t have only that amazing & astounding quality, venerable sir. I have another amazing & astounding quality.
an7.53:13.2 | S: there is another. | T: (none)
an7.53:13.3 | S: Of the five lower fetters taught by the Buddha, I don’t see any that I haven’t given up.” | T: Of the five lower fetters taught by the Blessed One, I don’t see in myself any that are unabandoned.”
an7.53:13.4 | S: “It’s incredible, Nanda’s Mother, it’s amazing!” | T: “It’s amazing, Nandamātar! It’s astounding!”
an7.53:14.1 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired Nanda’s Mother with a Dhamma talk, after which he rose from his seat and left. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta—having instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged the female lay follower Nandamātar with a talk on Dhamma—got up from his seat & left.
