## snp4.1
snp4.1:1.1 | S: If a mortal desires sensual pleasure | T: If one, longing for sensual pleasure,
snp4.1:1.2 | S: and their desire succeeds, | T: achieves it, yes,
snp4.1:1.3 | S: they definitely become elated, | T: he’s enraptured at heart.
snp4.1:1.4 | S: having got what they want. | T: The mortal gets what he wants.
snp4.1:2.1 | S: But for that personage in the throes of pleasure, | T: But if for that person
snp4.1:2.2 | S: aroused by desire, | T: —longing, desiring—
snp4.1:2.3 | S: when those pleasures fade, | T: the pleasures diminish,
snp4.1:2.4 | S: it inflicts them like an arrow strike. | T: he’s afflicted, as if shot with an arrow.
snp4.1:3.1 | S: One who, being mindful, | T: Whoever avoids sensual desires
snp4.1:3.2 | S: avoids sensual pleasures | T: (none)
snp4.1:3.3 | S: like side-stepping a snake’s head, | T: —as he would, with his foot, the head of a snake—
snp4.1:3.4 | S: transcends attachment to the world. | T: goes beyond, mindful, this attachment in the world.
snp4.1:4.1 | S: There are many objects of sensual desire: | T: A man who is greedy
snp4.1:4.2 | S: fields, lands, and gold; cattle and horses; | T: for fields, land, gold, cattle, horses,
snp4.1:4.3 | S: slaves and servants; women and relatives. | T: servants, employees, women, relatives, many sensual pleasures,
snp4.1:4.4 | S: When a man lusts over these, | T: (none)
snp4.1:5.1 | S: the weak overpower him | T: is overpowered with weakness
snp4.1:5.2 | S: and adversities crush him. | T: and trampled by trouble,
snp4.1:5.3 | S: Suffering follows him | T: for pain invades him
snp4.1:5.4 | S: like water in a leaky boat. | T: as water, a cracked boat.
snp4.1:6.1 | S: That’s why a personage, ever mindful, | T: So one, always mindful,
snp4.1:6.2 | S: should avoid sensual pleasures. | T: should avoid sensual desires.
snp4.1:6.3 | S: Give them up and cross the flood, | T: Letting them go, he’d cross over the flood
snp4.1:6.4 | S: as a bailed-out boat reaches the far shore. | T: like one who, having bailed out the boat, has reached the far shore.
snp4.1:6.5 | P: Kāmasuttaṁ paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## thag3.10
thag3.10:1.1 | S: In the past you had faith, | T: Before, you had faith.
thag3.10:1.2 | S: today you have none. | T: Today you don’t.
thag3.10:1.3 | S: What’s yours is yours alone— | T: What is yours is just for you.
thag3.10:1.4 | S: I’ve done nothing wrong. | T: I’ve done nothing wrong.
thag3.10:2.1 | S: Faith is impermanent, fickle: | T: For I’ve seen that faith is inconstant, wavering.
thag3.10:2.2 | S: or so I have seen. | T: (none)
thag3.10:2.3 | S: Passions wax and wane: | T: Even they who are passionate lose their passion.
thag3.10:2.4 | S: why would a sage waste away on that account? | T: Why should a sage waste away over that?
thag3.10:3.1 | S: The meal of a sage is cooked | T: A sage’s meal is cooked
thag3.10:3.2 | S: bit by bit in this family or that. | T: little by little, in this family and that.
thag3.10:3.3 | S: I’ll walk for alms, | T: I will go for alms.
thag3.10:3.4 | S: for my legs are strong. | T: I have the strength of my legs.
thag3.10:3.5 | P: … Sāṭimattiyo thero …. | T: (none)

## thag6.10
thag6.10:1.1 | S: I was only seven years old | T: When I was seven
thag6.10:1.2 | S: and had just gone forth | T: & newly gone forth,
thag6.10:1.3 | S: when I overcame the mighty serpent king | T: having conquered with my power the great powerful serpent,
thag6.10:1.4 | S: with my psychic powers. | T: (none)
thag6.10:2.1 | S: I brought water for my mentor | T: I was fetching water for my preceptor
thag6.10:2.2 | S: from the great lake Anotatta. | T: from the great lake, Anotatta,
thag6.10:2.3 | S: When he saw me, | T: when the Teacher saw me & said:
thag6.10:2.4 | S: my teacher declared: | T: (none)
thag6.10:3.1 | S: “Sāriputta, see this | T: “Look, Sāriputta, at that one,
thag6.10:3.2 | S: young boy coming, | T: the young boy coming there,
thag6.10:3.3 | S: carrying a water pot, | T: carrying a pot of water,
thag6.10:3.4 | S: serene inside himself. | T: well-centered within,
thag6.10:4.1 | S: His conduct inspires confidence, | T: his practices—inspiring;
thag6.10:4.2 | S: he is of lovely deportment: | T: his bearing—admirable.
thag6.10:4.3 | S: he is Anuruddha’s novice, | T: He’s Anuruddha’s novice,
thag6.10:4.4 | S: self-assured in psychic powers. | T: mature in his powers,
thag6.10:5.1 | S: Made a thoroughbred by a thoroughbred, | T: made thoroughbred by a thoroughbred,
thag6.10:5.2 | S: made good by the good, | T: good by one who is good,
thag6.10:5.3 | S: educated and trained by Anuruddha, | T: tamed by Anuruddha,
thag6.10:5.4 | S: who has completed his task. | T: trained by one whose task is done.
thag6.10:6.1 | S: Having attained ultimate peace | T: He, having reached the highest peace
thag6.10:6.2 | S: and witnessed the unshakable, | T: & realized the unshakable,
thag6.10:6.3 | S: that novice Sumana has the wish: | T: Sumana the novice wants this:
thag6.10:6.4 | S: ‘May no one find me out!’” | T: ‘Don’t let anyone know me.’”
thag6.10:6.5 | P: … Sumano thero …. | T: (none)

## thig6.3
thig6.3:1.1 | S: “You’re so young and beautiful! | T: Māra: “You, a beautiful young woman;
thig6.3:1.2 | S: I too am young, just a youth. | T: I, a young man, full of youth.
thig6.3:1.3 | S: Come, Khemā, let us enjoy | T: Come, Khemā, let’s enjoy ourselves
thig6.3:1.4 | S: the music of a five-piece band.” | T: to the music of a five-piece band.”
thig6.3:2.1 | S: “This body is rotting, | T: Khemā: “I’m disgusted, ashamed of this putrid body— disintegrating, dissolving.
