## sn16.13
sn16.13:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
sn16.13:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn16.13:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Mahākassapa went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Mahā Kassapa went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn16.13:1.4 | S: “What is the cause, sir, what is the reason why there used to be fewer training rules but more enlightened mendicants? | T: “What is the cause, lord, what is the reason, why before there were fewer training rules and yet more monks established in final gnosis,
sn16.13:1.5 | S: And what is the cause, what is the reason why these days there are more training rules and fewer enlightened mendicants?” | T: whereas now there are more training rules and yet fewer monks established in final gnosis?”
sn16.13:1.6 | S: “That’s how it is, Kassapa. When sentient beings are in decline and the true teaching is disappearing there are more training rules and fewer enlightened mendicants. | T: “That’s the way it is, Kassapa. When beings are degenerating and the true Dhamma is disappearing, there are more training rules and yet fewer monks established in final gnosis.
sn16.13:1.7 | S: The true teaching doesn’t disappear as long as the counterfeit of the true teaching hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: There is no disappearance of the true Dhamma as long as a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has not arisen in the world,
sn16.13:1.8 | S: But when the counterfeit of the true teaching appears in the world then the true teaching disappears. | T: but there is the disappearance of the true Dhamma when a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has arisen in the world.
sn16.13:2.1 | S: It’s like native gold, which doesn’t disappear as long as counterfeit gold hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: Just as there is no disappearance of gold as long as a counterfeit of gold has not arisen in the world,
sn16.13:2.2 | S: But when counterfeit gold appears in the world then native gold disappears. | T: but there is the disappearance of gold when a counterfeit of gold has arisen in the world,
sn16.13:2.3 | S: In the same way, the true teaching doesn’t disappear as long as the counterfeit of the true teaching hasn’t appeared in the world. | T: in the same way there is no disappearance of the true Dhamma as long as a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has not arisen in the world,
sn16.13:2.4 | S: But when the counterfeit of the true teaching appears in the world then the true teaching disappears. | T: but there is the disappearance of the true Dhamma when a counterfeit of the true Dhamma has arisen in the world.
sn16.13:3.1 | S: It’s not the elements of earth, water, fire, or air that make the true teaching disappear. | T: “It’s not the earth property that makes the true Dhamma disappear. It’s not the water property… the fire property… the wind property that makes the true Dhamma disappear.
sn16.13:3.2 | S: Rather, it’s the futile people who appear right here that make the true teaching disappear. | T: It’s worthless people who arise right here [within the Saṅgha] who make the true Dhamma disappear.
sn16.13:3.3 | S: The true teaching doesn’t disappear like a ship that sinks all at once. | T: The true Dhamma doesn’t disappear the way a ship sinks all at once.
sn16.13:3.4 | P: na kho, kassapa, evaṁ saddhammassa antaradhānaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn16.13:4.1 | S: There are five detrimental things that lead to the decline and disappearance of the true teaching. | T: “These five downward-leading qualities tend to the confusion and disappearance of the true Dhamma.
sn16.13:4.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn16.13:4.3 | S: It’s when the monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen lack respect and reverence for the Teacher, the teaching, the Saṅgha, the training, and immersion. | T: There is the case where the monks, nuns, male lay followers, & female lay followers live without respect, without deference, for the Teacher. They live without respect, without deference, for the Dhamma… for the Saṅgha… for the training… for concentration.
sn16.13:4.4 | S: These five detrimental things lead to the decline and disappearance of the true teaching. | T: These are the five downward-leading qualities that tend to the confusion and disappearance of the true Dhamma.
sn16.13:5.1 | S: There are five things that lead to the continuation, persistence, and enduring of the true teaching. | T: “But these five qualities tend to the stability, the non-confusion, the non-disappearance of the true Dhamma.
sn16.13:5.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn16.13:5.3 | S: It’s when the monks, nuns, laymen, and laywomen maintain respect and reverence for the Teacher, the teaching, the Saṅgha, the training, and immersion. | T: There is the case where the monks, nuns, male lay followers, & female lay followers live with respect, with deference, for the Teacher. They live with respect, with deference, for the Dhamma… for the Saṅgha… for the training… for concentration.
sn16.13:5.4 | S: These five things lead to the continuation, persistence, and enduring of the true teaching.” | T: These are the five qualities that tend to the stability, the non-confusion, the non-disappearance of the true Dhamma.”
